1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,590
In the lab, Aarav joins a resistor
and a capacitor to an AC supply.

2
00:00:04,890 --> 00:00:06,924
The resistor shows six volts.

3
00:00:06,924 --> 00:00:09,110
The capacitor shows eight volts.

4
00:00:09,410 --> 00:00:11,847
But the supply is only ten volts.

5
00:00:11,847 --> 00:00:13,192
Why not fourteen?

6
00:00:13,692 --> 00:00:16,368
Think of two friends
pulling a box with ropes.

7
00:00:16,718 --> 00:00:18,939
One pulls east with a force of six.

8
00:00:18,939 --> 00:00:20,963
The other pulls north with eight.

9
00:00:21,313 --> 00:00:23,455
The box does not feel fourteen.

10
00:00:23,455 --> 00:00:25,241
It moves along the slant.

11
00:00:25,591 --> 00:00:28,195
Six and eight at right angles make ten.

12
00:00:28,195 --> 00:00:29,740
Like a right triangle.

13
00:00:30,390 --> 00:00:32,491
In AC, voltages do the same.

14
00:00:32,491 --> 00:00:34,384
They peak at different times.

15
00:00:34,734 --> 00:00:36,521
The resistor's voltage peaks first.

16
00:00:36,521 --> 00:00:38,762
The capacitor's peaks
a quarter cycle later.

17
00:00:39,412 --> 00:00:41,593
First, how each part behaves on AC.

18
00:00:41,593 --> 00:00:43,077
Watch voltage and current.

19
00:00:43,427 --> 00:00:47,165
In a resistor, voltage and
current rise and fall together.

20
00:00:47,165 --> 00:00:48,514
They are in phase.

21
00:00:48,864 --> 00:00:53,182
In a capacitor, the current peaks
a quarter cycle before the voltage.

22
00:00:53,532 --> 00:00:55,577
Current leads voltage by ninety degrees.

23
00:00:55,927 --> 00:00:58,526
In an inductor, it is the other way round.

24
00:00:58,876 --> 00:01:00,943
Current lags voltage by ninety degrees.

25
00:01:01,593 --> 00:01:04,948
Now let's build a series RC
circuit on the breadboard.

26
00:01:05,298 --> 00:01:09,799
An AC supply of ten volts, a
resistor and a capacitor in a loop.

27
00:01:10,149 --> 00:01:12,636
We slow the wave down so we can watch it.

28
00:01:12,986 --> 00:01:18,047
The scope shows the resistor voltage,
the capacitor voltage and the supply.

29
00:01:18,397 --> 00:01:19,297
Look.

30
00:01:18,952 --> 00:01:23,371
The capacitor voltage peaks a quarter
cycle after the resistor voltage.

31
00:01:23,721 --> 00:01:26,986
At every instant, the two
do add up to the supply.

32
00:01:27,336 --> 00:01:29,310
But their peaks never line up.

33
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:31,481
So their sizes do not simply add.

34
00:01:32,131 --> 00:01:34,353
Now swap the capacitor for a coil.

35
00:01:34,353 --> 00:01:36,481
That makes a series RL circuit.

36
00:01:36,831 --> 00:01:40,041
Now the coil's voltage peaks a
quarter cycle before the resistor's.

37
00:01:40,691 --> 00:01:44,674
To add voltages that peak at
different times, we use arrows.

38
00:01:45,024 --> 00:01:46,740
These arrows are called phasors.

39
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:48,919
Their length is the size of the voltage.

40
00:01:49,269 --> 00:01:51,475
Draw the current as a flat arrow.

41
00:01:51,475 --> 00:01:53,491
It is the same in every part.

42
00:01:53,841 --> 00:01:57,531
The resistor's voltage, VR, points
the same way as the current.

43
00:01:58,231 --> 00:02:00,784
The capacitor's voltage,
VC, points straight down.

44
00:02:00,784 --> 00:02:01,922
Ninety degrees behind.

45
00:02:02,622 --> 00:02:04,383
Add the arrows tip to tail.

46
00:02:04,383 --> 00:02:06,278
The total is the slanted side.

47
00:02:06,978 --> 00:02:12,346
By Pythagoras, V equals the square
root of VR squared plus VC squared.

48
00:02:13,046 --> 00:02:16,779
Root of thirty six plus sixty
four is root of hundred.

49
00:02:16,779 --> 00:02:17,679
Ten volts.

50
00:02:18,328 --> 00:02:21,002
Now divide every side by the current.

51
00:02:21,002 --> 00:02:22,964
Volts per ampere are ohms.

52
00:02:23,314 --> 00:02:25,182
VR over I is R.

53
00:02:25,182 --> 00:02:29,355
VC over I is the capacitive reactance, XC.

54
00:02:30,055 --> 00:02:33,048
The total opposition is the impedance, Z.

55
00:02:33,048 --> 00:02:36,285
Z equals root of R
squared plus XC squared.

56
00:02:36,985 --> 00:02:40,556
The angle phi tells how
far the current is ahead.

57
00:02:40,556 --> 00:02:42,687
Tan phi equals XC over R.

58
00:02:43,387 --> 00:02:47,626
For an RL circuit, use the
inductive reactance, XL.

59
00:02:47,976 --> 00:02:51,597
Z equals root of R
squared plus XL squared.

60
00:02:51,597 --> 00:02:53,937
Tan phi equals XL over R.

61
00:02:54,637 --> 00:02:57,393
But here the current
lags behind the voltage.

62
00:02:58,093 --> 00:02:59,981
Cos phi equals R over Z.

63
00:02:59,981 --> 00:03:02,077
We call it the power factor.

64
00:03:02,727 --> 00:03:06,588
In AC, voltages add like
arrows, not like plain numbers.

65
00:03:07,388 --> 00:03:08,512
Worked example.

66
00:03:08,512 --> 00:03:11,714
A sixty ohm resistor in
series with a capacitor.

67
00:03:12,064 --> 00:03:14,178
Its reactance is eighty ohms.

68
00:03:14,178 --> 00:03:16,027
The supply is ten volts.

69
00:03:16,377 --> 00:03:19,789
Z equals root of sixty
squared plus eighty squared.

70
00:03:20,139 --> 00:03:21,847
That is one hundred ohms.

71
00:03:22,497 --> 00:03:24,151
Current equals V over Z.

72
00:03:24,151 --> 00:03:27,119
Ten over one hundred is
zero point one amperes.

73
00:03:27,769 --> 00:03:31,312
VR is zero point one
times sixty, six volts.

74
00:03:31,312 --> 00:03:32,974
VC is eight volts.

75
00:03:33,624 --> 00:03:35,598
Tan phi is eighty over sixty.

76
00:03:35,598 --> 00:03:37,765
Phi is about fifty three degrees.

77
00:03:38,415 --> 00:03:42,683
The power factor is sixty over
one hundred, zero point six.

78
00:03:43,333 --> 00:03:44,797
Now, the exam trap.

79
00:03:45,147 --> 00:03:47,821
Many students add the
voltages like numbers.

80
00:03:47,821 --> 00:03:49,590
Six plus eight is fourteen.

81
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:50,966
That is wrong.

82
00:03:50,966 --> 00:03:52,601
They are at right angles.

83
00:03:52,601 --> 00:03:53,617
Use Pythagoras.

84
00:03:53,617 --> 00:03:54,517
Ten volts.

85
00:03:55,057 --> 00:03:56,567
Where is this asked?

86
00:03:56,567 --> 00:03:59,627
In PUC, CBSE and ISC board papers.

87
00:03:59,977 --> 00:04:04,329
And in JEE Main, for example in
April twenty nineteen, second shift.

88
00:04:04,679 --> 00:04:09,365
Find the impedance, the current,
the phase angle or the power factor.

89
00:04:10,015 --> 00:04:10,915
Try this.

90
00:04:10,796 --> 00:04:15,321
A fifty ohm resistor and a coil are
in series across a hundred volt, fifty

91
00:04:15,321 --> 00:04:16,293
hertz supply.

92
00:04:16,293 --> 00:04:18,055
The current is one ampere.

93
00:04:18,055 --> 00:04:20,551
Find the coil's reactance and the phase.

94
00:04:25,951 --> 00:04:26,851
B.

95
00:04:26,322 --> 00:04:30,416
Eighty six point six ohms, with the
current lagging by sixty degrees.

96
00:04:30,766 --> 00:04:32,856
First, Z equals V over I.

97
00:04:32,856 --> 00:04:35,607
A hundred over one is a hundred ohms.

98
00:04:35,957 --> 00:04:39,245
XL equals root of Z
squared minus R squared.

99
00:04:39,245 --> 00:04:41,758
That is eighty six point six ohms.

100
00:04:42,108 --> 00:04:44,074
Cos phi equals R over Z.

101
00:04:44,074 --> 00:04:46,593
Fifty over a hundred is one half.

102
00:04:46,593 --> 00:04:48,493
So phi is sixty degrees.

103
00:04:48,843 --> 00:04:51,846
It is a coil, so the
current lags the voltage.

104
00:04:52,496 --> 00:04:54,043
Screenshot this card.

105
00:04:57,243 --> 00:05:00,099
Next, low pass and high pass RC filters.

106
00:05:00,099 --> 00:05:02,606
How does a circuit pick out the bass?
